
  - [1mIdentity module + login only[0m — one small module owning get/set/clear of the ID, and [38;5;153mlogin[39m as its first consumer.
    Other forms adopt it later by importing it. Buys you a real seam at the point where you know exactly one usage
    pattern. Costs: the second form may reveal the interface needs adjusting — but adjusting one module with one
    consumer is cheap.
  - [1mIdentity module + a shared form-submit wrapper now[0m — additionally build the generic "inject userId into any form
    submission" layer. Buys you drop-in reuse when the next form arrives. Costs: you're designing a general interface
    against one example, which is where the wrong abstraction usually gets locked in.
  - [1mParameter only, no module[0m — add [38;5;153muserId[39m to [38;5;153mlogin[39m, store it inline in the handler, generalize when a second form
    appears. Buys you the smallest diff. Costs: the storage/clearing logic ends up in the submit handler, and the second
    form copies it rather than reusing it.

  The module boundary is the thing worth getting right — it's what makes the other two options convertible. Building the
  wrapper now is the one that's expensive to undo.

  [1mMy recommendation: identity module + login only.[0m It gives you the shared, persistent seam you asked for without
  inventing a reuse interface against a single example. The second form is when you'll actually know what the wrapper
  should look like.

  One gap I'll flag: there is no logout anywhere in this app today. "Cleared on logout" therefore means the module
  exposes a [38;5;153mclear()[39m that a future logout calls — I'm not building a logout flow unless you want one.
